  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThis morning, a mid-level ridge over the Gulf Coast is resulting in a \nnear zonal flow over much of the upper US with a developing trough \nover the Rocky Mountain region. Widespread showers are being \nobserved upstream of the area as an upper level shortwave provides \nenergy for development on the northern periphery of the \naforementioned ridge. This pattern will slowly drift east, resulting \nin the showers to gradually spread east across the area today. \nInitial showers this morning are expected to only impact the \nwestern/southwestern counties as development and storm motion \nfollows the instability gradient. However, with a weakening high \npressure centered over the northern Great Lakes pushes east, a warm \nfront associated with a surface low over Midwest will be able to \nnudge further north towards the CWA. This boundary will likely \nbecome the focus for additional shower development and will \nultimately act as a path that the low pressure will follow into the \nsouthern Great Lakes region. As this boundary and the low get closer \nto the area tonight, showers are expected to become widespread \nacross the entire area with periods of heavy rain possible \novernight. On Sunday, this low will drag a strong cold front \nsoutheast through the area, again enhancing shower and possible \nthunderstorms development along it. Conditions should begin to dry \nout from north to south on Sunday afternoon as this boundary pushes \nthrough.\n\nThere remains some uncertainty with this forecast, especially in \nregards to how far north the aforementioned warm front will lift, \nhowever confidence continue to grow in regards to the potential for \nheavy rainfall and the potential for localized flooding. The current \nforecast through Sunday afternoon has 1-1.5 inches of rain falling \nalong a line from Cleveland to Youngstown and west with portions of \nfar NE OH and NW PA expected to get between 0.75-1 inch of rainfall. \nDeepening warm cloud layers and anonymously high PWAT values are \nexpected to couple with storm motion parallel to the flow and result \nin showers training. In areas impacted by this training, high QPF \nvalues can be expected. To highlight this concern, WPC has placed \nmuch of the area in a Day 1 Slight (2 of 4) ERO with portions of NE \nOH and NW PA in a Day 1 Marginal (1 of 4) ERO. Given the potential \nfor showers, widespread cloud cover, and the area likely staying \nnorth of the warm front, there is minimal risk of severe weather \nalthough a few rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out. High \ntemperatures today and Sunday will climb into the 70s with overnight \nlows tonight dropping into the 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAn active weather pattern is expected to persist through much of \nnext week as multiple midlevel troughs move east across the northern \nUS, bringing the chance for multiple rounds of precipitation and the \nfirst real taste of fall. Some models have continued to suggest a \ncut off low developing over the region midweek and persisting \nthrough late week. This pattern would support a northerly flow, \nkeeping cool temperatures around, but given model divergence this \nfar out and the inherent uncertainty, timing of any precipitation \nafter midweek and the exact temperatures may adjust some in coming \nforecasts. The current forecast suggests that highs beginning on \nMonday into next weekend will be in the 60s with overnight lows \ndropping into the 50s with patches in the 40s across NWPA.",
